{\b CORRECTION TO BUG SPRINT 7 — ADD S7-4: Photo import auto-resolve timing fix}\par
\par
The earlier analysis of this bug was wrong. Photo OCR IS capturing item names correctly (Apple, Rice, Carrot all extracted accurately). The bug is a SwiftData query timing issue, not AI quality.\par
\par
Root cause: SmartSalesPhotoView is presented as a sheet-inside-a-sheet (SalesView -> SmartSalesEntryView -> SmartSalesPhotoView). When AI analysis completes, step switches to .review and SaleEntryReviewView is created fresh inline. SaleEntryReviewView has @Query private var allItems: [InventoryItem]. The .onAppear fires immediately — but the @Query has not yet delivered its first results on that runloop tick. So autoResolveRows() runs against an empty allItems array: nothing maps.\par
\par
For PDF this doesn't happen because the analysis takes 10-20 seconds. By the time step = .review fires, the @Query has long since settled and allItems is populated.\par
\par
The same timing issue does NOT affect autoResolveRows() in SmartSalesCSVView because the user manually taps through steps (step 0 -> 1 -> 2 -> review), giving the query time to settle.\par
\par
{\b S7-4: Fix autoResolveRows() timing in SaleEntryReviewView}\par
\par
File: AITest/Views/SaleEntryReviewView.swift\par
\par
Location: In SaleEntryReviewView.body, find the existing .onAppear modifier:\par
.onAppear \{ autoResolveRows() \}\par
\par
Add .onChange(of: allItems) immediately after it:\par
.onAppear \{ autoResolveRows() \}\par
.onChange(of: allItems) \{ _, _ in autoResolveRows() \}\par
\par
This ensures autoResolveRows() re-fires the moment the @Query delivers its first results, regardless of which frame that happens in. The guard at the top of the loop (rows[index].resolvedItem == nil) prevents double-mapping rows that were already resolved.\par
\par
No other changes needed. The AI prompt is already correct — it passes knownItemNames and uses inventoryContext. The autoResolveRows() logic is already correct. The only issue is timing.\par
\par
Also: check SmartSalesPDFView and SmartSalesCSVView — if SaleEntryReviewView is presented inline (not as a sheet), apply the same .onChange(of: allItems) fix there too, as a safety net.\par
